{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1499993-how-many-characters-is-too-much","id":1499993,"title":"How many Characters is \"too much\"?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-12-17T17:17:00-08:00","id":1499993,"title":"How many Characters is \"too much\"?","content":"So, when thinking about creating a story and developing it through the course of a great chronicle, do you believe there's a certain &quot;ideal number&quot; of NPC characters in a story? I was arguing with a colleague, and he's of the opinion we should keep the number limited to at most half a dozen important NPCs as he argues too many would &quot;make the players forget about them and not build a strong enough connection with them&quot;. While I like the idea of taking a &quot;Game of Thrones&quot; approach to stories, i.e. character's driven and full of deep characters, with detailed backstory, etc. as I want to &quot;give life&quot; to the world and story I'm creating. As such, I think that the story only gains from a good number of characters with a good backstory and drama etc.<br /> <br /> I'd like to know your opinion on this matter. How has been your experience with games so far? Have you ever suffered from having way too many characters or the opposite, having perhaps too few? Do you think a game can really suffer from having many &quot;deep characters&quot;? How do you think it affects the game you're storytelling? Is there a balance or an ideal number of sorts? I'm really interested in hearing similar or different approaches to the subject.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12348,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","user":"Herr Meister","date":". 12-17-2022, 09:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-17T18:08:30-08:00","id":1499995,"title":"","content":"Me personally, I think ten or so is ideal. Ten gives you: <br /> The Ruler<br /> The Sycophant<br /> The Enforcer<br /> The Loyal Opposition<br /> The Informant<br /> The Agent of Chaos<br /> The Guru<br /> Three Randoms the players can push or pull as they see fit. <br /> <br /> One thing I'm a stickler on doing is designing all of the power players in a city and letting the PC's interact with them as they please. If the PC's want to spend more time with Bob the Brujah Biker and his cadre, the Sons of Caine, cool. If they prefer Victoria the Ventrue who's all about the Crypto, then it looks like Bob gets shoved to the background. Each power player has an agenda that goes on in the background, whether the PC's are aware of it or not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27252-hades","name":"Hades","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-17T18:19:41-08:00","id":1499996,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hades</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1499993-how-many-characters-is-too-much?p=1499995#post1499995\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Me personally, I think ten or so is ideal. Ten gives you:<br /> The Ruler<br /> The Sycophant<br /> The Enforcer<br /> The Loyal Opposition<br /> The Informant<br /> The Agent of Chaos<br /> The Guru<br /> Three Randoms the players can push or pull as they see fit.<br /> <br /> One thing I'm a stickler on doing is designing all of the power players in a city and letting the PC's interact with them as they please. If the PC's want to spend more time with Bob the Brujah Biker and his cadre, the Sons of Caine, cool. If they prefer Victoria the Ventrue who's all about the Crypto, then it looks like Bob gets shoved to the background. Each power player has an agenda that goes on in the background, whether the PC's are aware of it or not.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This is something I also do a lot, perhaps more than I should. Some players really love to interact with NPCs and sometimes they spend a lot of time dealing with a particular character and even though I love the fact that my players are interested in the characters I create, sometimes I need to &quot;force stop&quot; the scene somehow because if I let the player keep going with the scene they're going to spend hours on it and the other players are probably going to get bored lol.<br /> <br /> Then, of course there are just so many characters the players can interact with on a given game session, but I like to create and detail my NPCs and their actions, so I usually have a much larger &quot;cast&quot;, so to speak. Naturally, many won't often appear on some game's sessions, but they're certainly pulling some strings behind the scenes. I think this way the game feels more &quot;alive&quot; and &quot;real&quot;. I often tell my friends that if they don't do something then it's likely a NPC is going to do it anyway and make things happen, because my game doesn't stop lol","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12348,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","user":"Herr Meister","date":". 12-17-2022, 09:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-17T18:51:15-08:00","id":1499999,"title":"","content":"There's no ideal number of NPCs; just what ever works for your story and that's all up to you. Vampire, dealing with mortals, is more of a character-driven game with plots etc. vs, say, D&amp;D, which is more monster and adventure-driven. Politics should be a given. Back in the day, I've gone as far as to make a, &quot;Conspiracy-theorist&quot;, charge with photocopies of character-sketches detailing who's who in the city and what they want, complete with connecting-lines of string.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-17T19:16:43-08:00","id":1500003,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iguazu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1499993-how-many-characters-is-too-much?p=1499999#post1499999\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Back in the day, I've gone as far as to make a, &quot;Conspiracy-theorist&quot;, charge with photocopies of character-sketches detailing who's who in the city and what they want, complete with connecting-lines of string.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This is something I like and do on a regular basis (it's a relief to know I'm not the only one doing it, sometimes I think I'm a bit crazy/obsessed with the stories I create lol). I agree that there isn't an idea number of NPCs, I'm more interested in this you posted, for instance. To know with people's experience with this aspect of the game. I feel that I love a character's driven game, but perhaps that isn't the rule, I mean this topic is more of an exchange of experiences as ST that can be useful for people regarding this very important aspect of the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-17T23:13:33-08:00","id":1500013,"title":"","content":"In my experience, players are very goal oriented as opposed to character oriented, so those characters not directly (and obviously) related to their goals get overlooked unless there is something about them that grabs their interest.<br /> <br /> In addition, they have limited interested in even those characters they do care about. If someone is an opponent, they care about their capabilities and motives, not any convoluted backstories. Players in an RPD approach the game much differently than a reader approaching a book. Things a reader would love in learning about a particular antagonist becomes irrelevant if that same person runs a PC in an RPG.<br /> <br /> I don't think the game suffers if the ST gives detailed backstories of many dozens of NPCs. However, I do think it'd be a lot of unnecessary work as so much of that characterization will never come into play. The optimization of time spent developing the chronicle and NPCs and the actual value to the PCs tends towards less work, not more in my experience. After a certain point of key NPCs, an ST would be better off utilizing his time in other aspects of chronicle preparation than character development.<br /> <br /> Do the minimum necessary to figure out the NPCs role and motivation in the chronicle, and then only fill in the details if the players show actual interest in that character.<br /> <br /> If you figure out how to do that, please let me know because I definitely spend a lot more time on NPCs than is needed in my own chronicles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-17T23:37:47-08:00","id":1500014,"title":"","content":"When I'm coming up with a Chronicle, especially one I plan to have last for a time, I'll try and come up with around 15-20 NPCs. I'll make sure there's some sort of ties between those characters, and then some of them will have logical larger ties to other groups (the Sabbat, the True Black Hand, criminal groups, a blood cult, whatever) so that if I wanted to pull those groups into the story at a later point, it'll make some sort of sense. If werewolves get pulled into the story at some point, there will already be an NPC who has a history of some sort with lupines that could end up helping or hindering the PCs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-18T00:44:11-08:00","id":1500018,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1499993-how-many-characters-is-too-much?p=1500013#post1500013\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my experience, players are very goal oriented as opposed to character oriented, so those characters not directly (and obviously) related to their goals get overlooked unless there is something about them that grabs their interest.<br /> <br /> In addition, they have limited interested in even those characters they do care about. If someone is an opponent, they care about their capabilities and motives, not any convoluted backstories. Players in an RPD approach the game much differently than a reader approaching a book. Things a reader would love in learning about a particular antagonist becomes irrelevant if that same person runs a PC in an RPG.<br /> <br /> I don't think the game suffers if the ST gives detailed backstories of many dozens of NPCs. However, I do think it'd be a lot of unnecessary work as so much of that characterization will never come into play. The optimization of time spent developing the chronicle and NPCs and the actual value to the PCs tends towards less work, not more in my experience. After a certain point of key NPCs, an ST would be better off utilizing his time in other aspects of chronicle preparation than character development.<br /> <br /> Do the minimum necessary to figure out the NPCs role and motivation in the chronicle, and then only fill in the details if the players show actual interest in that character.<br /> <br /> If you figure out how to do that, please let me know because I definitely spend a lot more time on NPCs than is needed in my own chronicles.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yes, indeed. I surely understand that what often has appeal to readers doesn't apply to players in an RPG many times and I agree that more often than not, players are more goal oriented. One good thing I notice as well is that there are actually some players who really enjoy getting to know NPCs and really appreciate when some characters in the story are well-developed, etc. As I mentioned before, this has happened to me many times, with players even spending too much time talking and getting to know NPCs, even to the point where I have to find ways to shorten this time. <br /> <br /> I, however, always share your fear that I'm perhaps wasting my time investing so heavily on NPCs, thinking that this time could be best used elsewhere. This is something I always struggle to deal with, because while I love to develop the game story and especially the characters' stories, at the same time I hate when this time doesn't feel rewarded later when PCs don't make use of any of the backstory I created, especially because, as we all know, players love to do things we haven't anticipated lol. One thing I started to think about since some time ago, is that I can try to make use the investment I give to the creation of NPCs as inspiration for my writing, this way I don't feel like I'm wasting too much time, but rather working on something that can bear fruits in the future. At least this thought is somewhat comforting, hahaha...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12348,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","user":"Herr Meister","date":". 12-18-2022, 03:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-18T00:51:09-08:00","id":1500020,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1499993-how-many-characters-is-too-much?p=1500014#post1500014\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When I'm coming up with a Chronicle, especially one I plan to have last for a time, I'll try and come up with around 15-20 NPCs. I'll make sure there's some sort of ties between those characters, and then some of them will have logical larger ties to other groups (the Sabbat, the True Black Hand, criminal groups, a blood cult, whatever) so that if I wanted to pull those groups into the story at a later point, it'll make some sort of sense. If werewolves get pulled into the story at some point, there will already be an NPC who has a history of some sort with lupines that could end up helping or hindering the PCs.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> These are good things to have in mind as well, for sure. The connection NPCs have with other larger groups is very important when considering a story, as almost no character (un)lives in complete isolation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-18T01:06:24-08:00","id":1500022,"title":"","content":"A good way to look at NPC creation might be to start with major characters, who potentially have other NPCs below or around them. The characters are all nominally allied, at least on the surface, which is why they're being grouped together. But if PCs dig in more, they may discover the different constituent parts of the group have their own baggage, priorities, and relationships.<br /> <br /> Examples:<ul><li>The Ventrue Primogen and their brood</li> <li>The Brujah biker and their gang</li> <li>The Malkavian &quot;street seer&quot; and their following of mortal transients</li> <li>The Tremere Regent and their chantry</li> <li>The Nosferatu informant for the local warren</li> <li>The Inconnu Monitor and their acolytes</li> <li>Etc, etc.</li> </ul>The exact arrangement is context dependent, and the NPCs clustering around the central one can be inferiors, superiors, equals, etc. Bound by common cause, sect or bloodline affiliation, magical or interpersonal ties, obligation, or brute pragmatism. The important bit is the NPC the party meets will be their nominal introduction to their group. The contact that can go between the PCs and the related NPCs. The players will only learn about or even meet the ancillary NPCs to the degree they take interest in them. Like peeling a social onion.<br /> <br /> Of course, no person is an island, and even groups the party shows little interest in can be MADE relevant through social entanglements that cross factional bounds. You know, just in case you made an NPC the party didn't get a chance to meet, and you'd really rather they did. Just make them an ally, enemy, or something else to one or more characters within a faction they ARE dealing with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-18T09:39:59-08:00","id":1500049,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1499993-how-many-characters-is-too-much?p=1500022#post1500022\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A good way to look at NPC creation might be to start with major characters, who potentially have other NPCs below or around them. The characters are all nominally allied, at least on the surface, which is why they're being grouped together. But if PCs dig in more, they may discover the different constituent parts of the group have their own baggage, priorities, and relationships.<br /> <br /> Examples:<ul><li>The Ventrue Primogen and their brood</li> <li>The Brujah biker and their gang</li> <li>The Malkavian &quot;street seer&quot; and their following of mortal transients</li> <li>The Tremere Regent and their chantry</li> <li>The Nosferatu informant for the local warren</li> <li>The Inconnu Monitor and their acolytes</li> <li>Etc, etc.</li> </ul>The exact arrangement is context dependent, and the NPCs clustering around the central one can be inferiors, superiors, equals, etc. Bound by common cause, sect or bloodline affiliation, magical or interpersonal ties, obligation, or brute pragmatism. The important bit is the NPC the party meets will be their nominal introduction to their group. The contact that can go between the PCs and the related NPCs. The players will only learn about or even meet the ancillary NPCs to the degree they take interest in them. Like peeling a social onion.<br /> <br /> Of course, no person is an island, and even groups the party shows little interest in can be MADE relevant through social entanglements that cross factional bounds. You know, just in case you made an NPC the party didn't get a chance to meet, and you'd really rather they did. Just make them an ally, enemy, or something else to one or more characters within a faction they ARE dealing with.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I like these ideas, it's a good idea to create these groups beforehand, but of course these things can also be developed later, as the PCs interacts more with the aforementioned groups, for instance, they discover more things etc. What interest me more about what you said is the idea to make an NPC we spent some time working on relevant later, even if the PCs didn't interact with them before, we can reuse them later, as part of a group, etc. This way we avoid the feeling that &quot;we've lost time developing a character that isn't going to be used in the story&quot;. All great ideas, indeed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-19T07:51:21-08:00","id":1500097,"title":"","content":"A story should only have as many characters as is needed to tell the story.<br /> <br /> For every writer who creates a truly in-depth world with interesting characters who interact with each other, there are a dozen writers who just stack random side characters into every nook and cranny of the story with no real benefit and often to the reader's detriment. So, making worthwhile characters is its own skill.<br /> <br /> In RPGs the number of characters is fluid because despite you as the ST crafting a set of memorable characters with interconnected backstories and plots, the PCs will inevitably become obsessed with a random no-name NPC that you threw together on the spot to give a scene some life. So regardless of how many NPCs you originally planned for, the PCs will always figure out a way to add more to the story. Best to keep the number of NPCs low and leave yourself some room in case the roster grows.<br /> <br /> Template character groups are a mixed bag in my opinion. This makes things very easy to produce in a short amount of time, but it also means the content is shallow and unrefined for the games needs. Template groups are to RPGs what frozen dinners are to home cooking, fine in a pinch, but unfulfilling and potentially unhealthy in the long run. Don't get me wrong though, there is nothing wrong with taking a template group and slotting it in as a place holder while you refine your ideas, but if that refinement never happens or the concept is never revisited, then the game design can fall apart.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-19T11:17:31-08:00","id":1500109,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1499993-how-many-characters-is-too-much?p=1500097#post1500097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A story should only have as many characters as is needed to tell the story.<br /> <br /> For every writer who creates a truly in-depth world with interesting characters who interact with each other, there are a dozen writers who just stack random side characters into every nook and cranny of the story with no real benefit and often to the reader's detriment. So, making worthwhile characters is its own skill.<br /> <br /> In RPGs the number of characters is fluid because despite you as the ST crafting a set of memorable characters with interconnected backstories and plots, the PCs will inevitably become obsessed with a random no-name NPC that you threw together on the spot to give a scene some life. So regardless of how many NPCs you originally planned for, the PCs will always figure out a way to add more to the story. Best to keep the number of NPCs low and leave yourself some room in case the roster grows.<br /> <br /> Template character groups are a mixed bag in my opinion. This makes things very easy to produce in a short amount of time, but it also means the content is shallow and unrefined for the games needs. Template groups are to RPGs what frozen dinners are to home cooking, fine in a pinch, but unfulfilling and potentially unhealthy in the long run. Don't get me wrong though, there is nothing wrong with taking a template group and slotting it in as a place holder while you refine your ideas, but if that refinement never happens or the concept is never revisited, then the game design can fall apart.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Reading your reply made me think about how those templates for creating characters of a given type are helpful. Especially the ones that comes with traits (and not just points to invest), that can save a lot of time when creating scenes where there's only one or two NPCs of real relevance and the rest are just there to fill the scene, e.g. the Justicar and his archons. I shouldn't feel the need to give traits to every single archon for instance. <br /> <br /> Other than that, I agree that many stories can and do suffer from a too numerous group of characters (some people think about &quot;Fantastic Beasts: The Crimes of Grindelwald&quot; as a good example of that). Creating (and giving life to) good characters is surely an art. Funny thing you mentioned is that players often get a weird interest in NPCs that have NOTHING to do with the story lol, this has happened many times over the years in my games. Sometimes it really pays off, as I get to develop more an NPC that previously shouldn't have any relevant role in the story.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-19T13:17:28-08:00","id":1500128,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1499993-how-many-characters-is-too-much?p=1500097#post1500097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In RPGs the number of characters is fluid because despite you as the ST crafting a set of memorable characters with interconnected backstories and plots, the PCs will inevitably become obsessed with a random no-name NPC that you threw together on the spot to give a scene some life.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep, you never know what NPCs will become important in a chronicle. I try to provide some level of flavor text, no matter how minor, to NPCs a player may interact with, and it is surprising how providing one little innocent detail makes a player interested in that character, and you must create background on the fly as the relationship becomes more important. In some cases the only reason that NPC existed was I needed some flavor text background for that scene and wasn't even a real character.<br /> <br /> While I may describe certain important NPCs in full detail, for the rest I try to keep them within the &quot;index card rule.&quot; Everything important about a character - description, motive, history, backgrounds, key dice pools, etc. - must fit on a 3x5 inch index card and have room left over. That boils everything down to their essentials. I may not need anything more. And if that NPC for whatever reason becomes more important, I can easily build the character up as needed.<br /> <br /> I don't have index cards for every kind of character - many ghouls or other retainers might only be a brief phrase on some other character's index card, or might only just be an undescribed &quot;dot&quot; that I create on the fly for an unexpected scene. But if they do become more important, they may get their own index card or even full write up at some time.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-19T13:44:18-08:00","id":1500134,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1499993-how-many-characters-is-too-much?p=1500128#post1500128\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yep, you never know what NPCs will become important in a chronicle. I try to provide some level of flavor text, no matter how minor, to NPCs a player may interact with, and it is surprising how providing one little innocent detail makes a player interested in that character, and you must create background on the fly as the relationship becomes more important. In some cases the only reason that NPC existed was I needed some flavor text background for that scene and wasn't even a real character.<br /> <br /> While I may describe certain important NPCs in full detail, for the rest I try to keep them within the &quot;index card rule.&quot; Everything important about a character - description, motive, history, backgrounds, key dice pools, etc. - must fit on a 3x5 inch index card and have room left over. That boils everything down to their essentials. I may not need anything more. And if that NPC for whatever reason becomes more important, I can easily build the character up as needed.<br /> <br /> I don't have index cards for every kind of character - many ghouls or other retainers might only be a brief phrase on some other character's index card, or might only just be an undescribed &quot;dot&quot; that I create on the fly for an unexpected scene. But if they do become more important, they may get their own index card or even full write up at some time.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Yes, &quot;one little innocent detail&quot; can make a player really interested in that character, and then I have to create more backstory for the character, build its relantionships, etc. That happened with me recently when I crafted a Black Fury Werewolf with an impotent voice (I try to make different voices when roleplays all my characters) and a certain attitude and one of my players got obsessed about her lol, so now I'm thinking about a way to give more room to her. <br /> <br /> You got me curious about this &quot;index card rule&quot;. That seems interesting when creating characters or just preparing a scene really. One important thing about RPG's stories that was mentioned here is that they tend to be always &quot;Fluid&quot;, never &quot;solid&quot;. It's almost never possible to imagine it as a &quot;solid&quot; thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-21T11:09:02-08:00","id":1500314,"title":"","content":"We try to keep it small in general in our games as creating a decent backstory for a character is much harder then most newbies realize.<br /> <br /> So far we have the following.<br /> <br /> The Baron whom is starting to come into her own and is better adminstatar then she thought.<br /> The Bodyguards whom make sure she is safe when make deals with other Kindred.<br /> The Friendly Hookup who can get you what you need or knows people who do.<br /> The Historian who has considerable academic knowledge and considerable age gives pause to some of the more rowdy sect members <br /> The Shadows who seem to know what is happening throughout the domain and are willing to share for a price.<br /> The Ambitous Mystics who want to safe place to learn there arts without to much hassle and maybe rise to promenice.<br /> The Devil at the gate who wants to punish them for personal reasons.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":27361,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","user":"Lysander","date":". 12-21-2022, 02:19 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-29T19:51:48-08:00","id":1501658,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1499993-how-many-characters-is-too-much?p=1500003#post1500003\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> This is something I like and do on a regular basis (it's a relief to know I'm not the only one doing it, sometimes I think I'm a bit crazy/obsessed with the stories I create lol). I agree that there isn't an idea number of NPCs, I'm more interested in this you posted, for instance. To know with people's experience with this aspect of the game. I feel that I love a character's driven game, but perhaps that isn't the rule, I mean this topic is more of an exchange of experiences as ST that can be useful for people regarding this very important aspect of the game.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's mostly so I can keep track of my NPC's. Most of the time, my NPCs were composites of Tales from the Crypt, characters. And especially for a Vampire game, it's very character-driven. It's about mortals, really.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-31T12:11:15-08:00","id":1501824,"title":"","content":"To me it really depends on the setting. I do like to stick to the book rules of 1:100,000 ratio for kindred to population. We often play smaller town so I really keep characters limited to the coterie, their allies/ghouls, and a handful of Kindred in town/larger cities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27749-welard","name":"Welard","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"21"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}